# Greenfield

EnvyBot has **not shipped**. The operator book and a bench/fleet of radios
are the only real environment. There is no field fleet to upgrade.

Do not design for legacy books, old yaml keys, or rolling schema.

## Do

- Change the canonical shape (`nodes.yaml` keys, sqlite columns, APIs, UI).
  Update in-repo callers and tests in the same change set.
- Delete dead keys, aliases, and ignore-paths. One name per concept.
- If a live book still has junk: one-shot inline Python in the session,
  confirm clean, **do not commit the script**.
- Treat a leftover `flood:` (or similar) as junk. Runtime reads the
  canonical key only.

## Don't

- Ship `migrate_*`, start-of-fleet yaml rewrites, or dual-read
  (old key + new key) "just in case."
- Keep deprecated apply/onboard/UI paths, leftover book keys, or
  ignore-lists for removed prefs (`fem_vfem`, `rxgain_keep`, …).
- Add compatibility shims because a prior commit used another name.
- Leave a migrator in the repo after a one-shot cleanup.

## Book vs product

| In product code | Out of product code |
|-----------------|---------------------|
| Load/write the current book shape | Rewriting books on `./envybot fleet` start |
| `strip_observed` in memory (telemetry is sqlite) | Deleting/renaming keys on every launch |
| Explicit operator edits (UI, onboard, apply persist) | Built-in "first run migrate" |

`migrate_legacy` (`polls.jsonl` → sqlite) is leftover ingest, not a
pattern. Do not copy it. Do not add a yaml twin.

